{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And they brought them back word.</b> They did not go to their tents but as soon as they arrived they went to the camp of Israel to inform them and to bring out an evil report, and the evil report was what they said: ״ארץ אוכלת יושביה היא״ — 'a land that consumes its inhabitants.' And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted that the Holy One, blessed be He, brought a plague upon the Canaanites at that time so that they would be occupied with burying their dead and not pay attention to the spies, and the spies would be able to inspect the land as they wished. And through the very miracles that were performed for them, they brought out an evil report, and they would frighten and terrify them with Amalek — this is what they said: ״עמלק יושב בארץ הנגב״ — to hint that there is no spirit to enter the land, for all of them are mighty: since Amalek is in the south, the Canaanite in the west and in the east, and the Amorite in the mountain. They reported their mission and discouraged the heart of the people by way of hint, out of fear of Moses and Aaron. Now Caleb silenced them and showed his opinion to be with them, wanting to speak against Moses, so that he would have room to speak among them, and he said: ״עלה נעלה וירשנו אותה״ — 'Let us go up and inherit it.' If they are strong, are we not stronger than them? And immediately the spies answered and said: ״לא נוכל לעלות אל העם כי חזק הוא ממנו״ — 'We cannot go up against the people, for it is stronger than we.' What had earlier been understood from the implication of their words, that they had spoken in hints, they now said explicitly. And that they said 'to go up against the people' and did not say 'we cannot come to the cities' — this was a way of exaggeration and evil report, for even against the people, if they came out to us in the field, we could not go up against them to fight them, let alone conquer from them great fortified cities. And the meaning of the language of 'going up' — and this in the field — so too the language of those who do battle in formation. And after they did evil and discouraged the heart of the people by hint, they further added to do evil with their words and deepened their destruction by saying: ״ארץ אוכלת יושביה היא״ — 'a land that consumes its inhabitants.' And they themselves had already testified that they are strong and mighty, and their words contradicted one another. And when they said, ״כי חזק הוא ממנו״ — 'for it is stronger than we' — this was an allusion on high, as it were, that the Master of the house cannot remove His vessels from there. They further added to alarm them and to terrify them by what they said: ״ושם ראינו את הנפילים״ — 'and there we saw the Nephilim.' For above they mentioned them in the language of ״ילידי הענק״ — 'children of the Anak' — and now they said 'the Nephilim,' whom everyone who sees them — his heart falls. And this is what he returned from the Nephilim, meaning the ancients from the time of Genesis, about whom it is said (Genesis 6:4): ״הנפילים היו בארץ״. And three names were called upon them: Nephilim, Anakim, Rephaim. The Nephilim were the heads of the families of those who were the 'sons of Elohim' mentioned in Genesis, and they were not like other people to gaze upon, and everyone who looks upon them — a great terror falls upon him. Afterward they diminished in strength and might and were called ענקים, because they would grant heat (?) in their stature, that is, to the one looking, for one was not permitted to gaze upon them, like one who gazes at a tall tower. Afterward, in the distant generations, as they rolled on and moved away from the might of those Nephilim, their strength was further diminished and weakened, and they were called רפאים. And Og king of Bashan was of the Rephaim and was the refuse of them, for so they said in Midrash Tanchuma: ״והוא נשאר מיתר הרפאים״ — and this is Og, who killed Amraphel and his companions, and he was the refuse of them, like the olive pits that are cast out from the pressing, as it is said (Genesis 14:13): ״ויבא הפליט״ — 'and the fugitive came' — this is Og. And the reason he merited to be saved from the Flood was in the merit of Abraham, and also in the merit of Israel, so that the Holy Land would be settled through men such as they. The upshot of the matter: they denied the wonders of HaShem, blessed be He, and His mighty deeds, and reverted to being of little faith, and they did not remember the miracles of Egypt and the miracles of the Sea and the miracles of the wilderness, nor the war with Amalek, about which it is written (Exodus 17:13): ״ויחלוש יהושע את עמלק ואת עמו״ — now they feared them in the matter of Amalek and said: ״עמלק יושב״. They further went to extremes to speak heresy in saying (Numbers 14:2–3): ״לו מתנו בארץ מצרים או במדבר הזה לו מתנו, ולמה ה׳ מביא אותנו אל הארץ הזאת לנפול בחרב נשינו וטפינו יהיו לבז הלא טוב לנו שוב מצרימה, נתנה ראש ונשובה מצרימה״. And the Holy One, blessed be He, said (Deuteronomy 17:16): ״לא תוסיפון לשוב בדרך הזה עוד״. And when Moses and Aaron heard all this, they fell on their faces and were ashamed with great shame, because they were profaning HaShem in public, and because everyone had followed the words of the spies. And about them King Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 26:6): ״מקצה רגלים חמס שותה שולח דברים ביד כסיל״ — 'he who sends a message by the hand of a fool drinks down violence' — one who is from the edge of feet to the edge, meaning that he draws them backward and does not go to his work, drinks violence; and so, 'he sends words by the hand of a fool.' And here Joshua and Caleb rent their garments for this, and he began to appease them: ״אם חפץ בנו ה׳ ... אך בה׳ אל תמרודו ... וה׳ אתנו אל תיראום״. And they hardened their faces and said to stone them with stones. See how many transgressions are drawn one after another, and how many kindnesses the Holy One, blessed be He, used to do for them, that He did not destroy them from the world for all these provocations, and that He did not withhold the manna from descending for them day by day."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {0 "On the phrase מעניקים חמה בקומתן: the meaning is uncertain; translated hyper-literally. The word חמה may mean 'heat,' 'sun,' or 'wrath,' and the precise sense Rabbeinu Bahya intends for מעניקים here is unclear. The parenthetical (לא) appears in the source and is retained."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:32:25.408548064Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}